# KEYMAP.md §Daemon-down surfaces (Doctor report) · UX-SPEC.md §3.12 Doctor — `D` in Settings
# raises the doctor table, and the report owns the whole window while it is up.
#
# The report has no snapshot field of its own, so it is proved by what it *takes*: raising it
# closes the Settings dialog it was raised from (`dialogs/settings/persistence.rs` — "the doctor
# table is a full surface; it replaces the dialog it was raised from"), and while it is up the
# context chain is `Daemon > Doctor`, so a key with no Doctor binding is dead. `J` is that key —
# it is bound in `Hub` and nowhere on the report — and it opens the Jobs panel again the moment
# `Esc` dismisses the report. This is the §3.8.6 About entry point, where the daemon is healthy,
# which is why the table has real checks in it rather than one failed row.
#
# Headless: virtual-lane only, because of its `shot`. The keyboard does reach the focused view
# in the headless lane (§7), so everything else here would run there.
fixture: busy
await idle 20000
assert overlay absent

key ,
await overlay == Dialog 5000
assert dialog.name == "Settings"

# `D` runs doctor. The answer replaces the dialog, which is the only overlay-visible proof that
# the full-window report took the surface.
key D
await overlay absent 30000
shot doctor

# `J` is `Hub`'s, and `Hub` is not on the chain while the report is up. `key` answers only after
# the frame it caused has painted, so an overlay that was going to open has already opened.
key J
assert overlay absent

# `Esc` dismisses the report and hands the Hub its keys back.
key escape
key J
await overlay == Jobs 5000
dump jobs-after-doctor
quit
